<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-6-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Review of Power Series </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Find the radius of convergence for Power Series </p></li>
            <li><p> Refactor power series </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-power-series">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that an infinite series of the form <md> \sum_{n=0}^\infty c_n (x-a)^n </md> is called a <alert>power series</alert> centered at <m>x=a</m>. We say it <alert> converges to <m>L</m> </alert> if <md> \lim_{N \to \infty} \sum_{n=0}^N c_n(a-x)^n = L.</md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-ratio-test">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the series <m> \sum_{n=0}^\infty a_n </m>. Suppose that <md> \lim_{n \to \infty} \left| \frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \right| = L. </md> The <alert> ratio test</alert> states that the sum <alert> converges</alert> if <m>L \lt 1</m> and <alert>divergest</alert> if <m>L \gt 1</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                We can use the following tricks to <alert>reindex</alert> power series.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    

</section>
